Exactly. I’d make the branding almost anti-branding: no readable logo at normal distance. If someone notices the object closely, then they discover the mark.

For the name, I’d stay away from anything that literally says “hair,” “clip,” “grooming,” or sounds overly tactical. It should sound like a designed object.

My strongest candidates:

* TRACE — probably my favorite. It’s there, but barely. The product itself leaves almost no visual trace.
* VEIL — disappears into the hair; elegant without feeling feminine.
* LOWLINE — architectural, masculine, describes the profile.
* HUSH — quiet object, quiet branding, clever.
* SLATE — material/design language; matte black, clean, masculine.
* NOTCH — mechanical and understated. Also relates naturally to the teeth.
* LATCH — brutally simple. Makes the thing feel like hardware rather than an accessory.
* NULL — almost invisible; very contemporary industrial-design feel.
* SHADE — blends into the hair, slightly more fashion-forward.
* SABLE — dark, premium, understated.
* OFFSET — interesting design/engineering language.
* FLUSH — because it sits nearly flush against the head. Actually pretty damn good.
* RECESS — intentionally withdrawn/inset.
* UNDER — odd enough to be cool; built around being beneath notice.
* QUIET — stripping “Quiet Lock” down to just QUIET makes it considerably cooler.

I think TRACE, FLUSH, and NOTCH are the three worth developing.

Branding treatment

You’re dead on about the monogram.

Imagine TRACE as the company/product family, but nowhere on the clip does it say TRACE.

Instead, the outer shell has something like a tiny T formed from two precision-cut grooves, maybe 3–4 mm tall.

Not printed.

Not silver.

Not filled with paint.

The exact same gunmetal material, except the mark is either:

micro-bead-blasted matte against satin metal, or one shade darker via anodizing/PVD.

So from five feet away: nothing.

From two feet away: maybe you catch a weird little line.

Someone holding it:

Oh shit. That’s a T.

That is much cooler.

And I’d put the actual wordmark inside the mechanism, where you only see it when you open the clip. Almost like the maker’s mark on a good knife or watch.

Outside = monogram.
Inside = identity.

For TRACE, I can already see the monogram being especially clever: just two tiny intersecting traces cut into the shell, almost like machining witness marks rather than a logo.

That actually reinforces the whole product philosophy: designed to disappear; rewarded when inspected.